 Cached JPEG data may occupy at most 12 GiB. Before admitting a completed sheet, evict enough existing sheets to remain within that bound. Temporary render output is kept separately and removed on completion or failure. Eviction never deletes source footage or changes job-ticket semantics.
 
-The eviction policy is still open. Candidates are LRU, frequency-based eviction with aging, and 2Q. LRU has little bookkeeping and follows recent work quickly but is vulnerable to sequential scans. Aged frequency counts protect repeatedly popular clips but add counter maintenance and can lag a project switch. 2Q separates first-use entries from reused entries, resisting scans at the cost of extra queues and a queue-size parameter. None changes the external interface, queue concurrency, or disk budget.
+### 2Q Eviction
+
+The worker maintains two resident queues over the same cache entries:
+
+- **Probationary queue** — FIFO, holding sheets requested once since admission. A newly rendered sheet enters at the tail.
+- **Protected queue** — LRU, holding sheets requested at least twice. A hit on a probationary sheet promotes it to the protected queue at the most-recently-used end. A hit on a protected sheet refreshes its recency.
+
+The protected queue is capped at 80% of the 12 GiB budget (about 9.6 GiB). Probationary sheets occupy the remainder of the cache, which can grow beyond 20% while the protected queue is under its cap. When a promotion would exceed the protected cap, the least-recently-used protected sheets are demoted to the probationary tail until the protected queue is within its cap.
+
+When admission requires space, the worker evicts from the probationary head first, oldest first-use sheet first. If the probationary queue cannot free enough space, it evicts from the protected LRU tail. A sequential screening therefore evicts its own one-use sheets instead of the comparison set, while a workload with no one-use pressure still lets reused sheets use most of the cache.
+
+Both queues account for JPEG bytes rather than entry counts, so the 12 GiB bound is enforced directly. Queue membership and all recency metadata are private to the worker; neither the review API nor job tickets observe or depend on them. Cache hits, promotions, and evictions do not change the 200/202/404 contract.
+
+## Decisions & Alternatives
+
+| Decision | Chosen | Alternatives Considered | Rationale |
+|----------|--------|------------------------|-----------|
+| Cache eviction policy | 2Q with probationary and protected queues | LRU; frequency-based eviction with aging | 2Q protects sheets that are requested repeatedly from a long one-use screening scan. LRU lets a sequential scan evict the comparison set. Aged frequency counts add more bookkeeping and can retain stale favorites across project switches. |
+| Protected queue share | 80% of the 12 GiB budget (about 9.6 GiB), starting value | Equal split; fixed absolute queue sizes; no protected cap | A large protected share keeps the repeated comparison set resident while probationary traffic uses spare capacity. The share is a pilot tuning parameter. |
+| Eviction victim order | Probationary FIFO head first, then protected LRU tail | Global LRU; global frequency | Evicting one-use sheets before reused sheets is the property that makes 2Q resist scans. A global policy cannot distinguish a first screening from a comparison session. |
+| Reuse history across eviction | Two resident queues only; no separate eviction history | 2Q with a ghost/history queue for evicted probationary keys | A sheet evicted before its second request re-enters probationary on its next request. Omitting a ghost queue keeps replacement state to two queues without a structure the pilot workload needs. |
+
+## Open Questions & Future Decisions
+
+### Resolved
+1. ✅ Eviction policy: 2Q, protecting repeatedly requested comparison sheets during long screening scans.
+
+### Deferred
+1. Tune the protected queue share from pilot hit rates; 80% is the starting value.
+2. Add a ghost/history queue if comparison sheets are frequently evicted before their second request.
